#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ifi rebuild-docker: a rebuild replaces this container, so it can't be done
# from inside it (there is no docker command in here). This prints the steps.
set -euo pipefail
source "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "$0")")/common.sh"

if ! in_container; then
    cat <<'TXT'
You're not in the container, so you can rebuild from right here. In the repo folder:
  docker compose down
  docker build -f Dockerfile.dev -t ifiplatform-web:dev .
  docker compose up -d
Using VS Code? Use Command Palette -> Dev Containers: Rebuild Container instead.
Your code, Git branches and database are kept.
TXT
    exit 0
fi

cat <<'TXT'
A rebuild makes a new container. It can't be started from this terminal,
because this terminal runs inside the container that gets replaced.

Do this in VS Code:
  1. Press Ctrl+Shift+P (Mac: Cmd+Shift+P)
  2. Run: Dev Containers: Rebuild Container
  3. Wait. VS Code reopens in the new container.

Your code, Git branches and database are kept. Extensions and packages you
installed by hand inside the container are not.

Still broken after that? Build from scratch: run this in a terminal on your
own computer (not in VS Code's container terminal), in the repo folder,
then rebuild in VS Code again:
  docker build --no-cache -f Dockerfile.dev -t ifiplatform-web:dev .
TXT
